{% extends 'base.html.twig' %}
{% block body_class %}user-guide{% endblock %}
{% block title %}User Guide – Hosts & Interfaces{% endblock %}

{% block content %}
{% set section_title = 'Hosts & Interfaces' %}
{{ include('user_guide/_breadcrumb.html.twig') }}

<div class="d-flex justify-content-between align-items-center mb-4">
    <h1 class="h3 mb-0"><i class="bi bi-pc-display me-2"></i>Hosts &amp; Interfaces</h1>
    <a href="{{ path('host_index') }}" class="btn btn-sm btn-outline-primary">
        <i class="bi bi-arrow-right-circle me-1"></i>Go to Hosts
    </a>
</div>

<div class="row g-4">
<div class="col-lg-9">

{# Overview #}
<div class="card mb-4" id="overview">
    <div class="card-header fw-semibold"><i class="bi bi-info-circle me-1"></i>Overview</div>
    <div class="card-body">
        <p>A <strong>host</strong> represents a physical or virtual device on your network — a server, workstation, printer, IoT device, or any other addressable endpoint. Each host has one or more <strong>network interfaces</strong>, and each interface can have a MAC address, an IPv4 address, an IPv6 address, and one or more DNS names.</p>
        <p class="mb-0">The data model looks like this:</p>
        <ul class="mt-2 mb-0">
            <li><strong>Host</strong> → name, location (building + room), tags, notes</li>
            <li><strong>Network Interface</strong> → MAC address, subnet assignment, IPv4/IPv6 addresses, DNS names</li>
            <li><strong>Virtual IP</strong> → floating IP address shared by member interfaces for VRRP/HSRP/Active-Gateway failover groups</li>
        </ul>
    </div>
</div>

{# Host list #}
<div class="card mb-4" id="host-list">
    <div class="card-header fw-semibold"><i class="bi bi-list-ul me-1"></i>The Host List</div>
    <div class="card-body">
        <p>The Hosts page (<strong>Hosts</strong> in the top nav) shows all active hosts in a <strong>collapsible tree view</strong>. Each host is a parent row, and its network interfaces appear as indented sub-rows directly beneath it.</p>
        <ul>
            <li>Hosts are <strong>expanded by default</strong>. Click the chevron (<i class="bi bi-chevron-down"></i>) on any host row to collapse its interfaces.</li>
            <li>Use the <strong>Collapse All</strong> and <strong>Expand All</strong> buttons (top-right of the action bar) to collapse or expand every host at once.</li>
            <li>Collapse state is <strong>saved in your browser</strong> and persists across page navigations.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Each host row shows the host name, optional location and Snipe-IT asset label, tags, last-modified date, and action buttons. Interface sub-rows show the interface name (with MAC address in parentheses), subnet, IPv4, IPv6, and DNS names. Interfaces that belong to a virtual IP group show an additional indented sub-row displaying the VIP label, protocol badge, and VIP IP address.</p>
        <p class="mb-0 text-muted small">Interfaces without a name display the MAC address as their identifier.</p>
    </div>
</div>

{# Search #}
<div class="card mb-4" id="search">
    <div class="card-header fw-semibold"><i class="bi bi-search me-1"></i>Searching for Hosts</div>
    <div class="card-body">
        <h6>Simple Search</h6>
        <p>Type in the search bar at the top of the host list. The simple search matches against host name, location (building / room), subnet name, IP address (IPv4 or IPv6), MAC address, and DNS name. Use <code>*</code> as a wildcard (e.g. <code>web*</code>, <code>10.0.*</code>).</p>

        <h6 class="mt-3">Condition Builder</h6>
        <p>Click <strong>Advanced</strong> to open the condition builder. The builder lets you compose precise filters using multiple fields, operators, and values — with full AND / OR support.</p>

        <p><strong>Structure:</strong> conditions within a group are AND'd together; groups are OR'd against each other.</p>
        <ul>
            <li>Add more conditions to a group with <strong>+ Add condition</strong>.</li>
            <li>Add a second group with <strong>+ Add OR group</strong> — results matching <em>any</em> group are returned.</li>
            <li>Remove a condition with the <i class="bi bi-x text-danger"></i> button. Remove an entire group with <strong>× Remove group</strong>.</li>
            <li>Click <strong>Search</strong> to apply the builder. Click <strong>Clear</strong> to reset everything.</li>
        </ul>

        <h6 class="mt-3">Available Fields</h6>
        <div class="table-responsive">
            <table class="table table-sm table-bordered">
                <thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>Operators</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr>
                        <td>Host Name</td>
                        <td>contains, does not contain, starts with, does not start with, ends with, does not end with, equals, does not equal</td>
                        <td>Use <code>*</code> as a wildcard in the value (e.g. <code>web*</code>)</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td>Building</td>
                        <td>is, is not</td>
                        <td>Select from a dropdown of buildings</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td>Room</td>
                        <td>contains, starts with, ends with, equals, and negatives</td>
                        <td>Free-text, supports wildcards</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td>Subnet</td>
                        <td>is, is not</td>
                        <td>Select from a dropdown; includes <em>— No subnet —</em> to find unassigned interfaces</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td>IP Address</td>
                        <td>contains, starts with, ends with, equals, and negatives</td>
                        <td>Matches IPv4 or IPv6; use <code>10.0.*</code> to match a range</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td>MAC Address</td>
                        <td>contains, starts with, ends with, equals, and negatives</td>
                        <td>Use <code>aa:bb:*</code> to match by OUI prefix</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td>DNS Name</td>
                        <td>contains, starts with, ends with, equals, and negatives</td>
                        <td>Matches both hostname and FQDN; use <code>*.example.com</code></td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td>Tag</td>
                        <td>is, is not (dropdown) <em>and</em> contains, starts with, ends with, equals, and negatives (text)</td>
                        <td><em>is / is not</em> lets you pick a known tag from a dropdown; text operators match against the tag name with wildcard support</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td>DHCP Mismatch</td>
                        <td>is true, is false</td>
                        <td><em>is true</em> returns hosts with at least one interface whose last DHCP lease IP falls outside its assigned subnet's CIDR; <em>is false</em> excludes those hosts</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td>Last DHCP</td>
                        <td>after, before, between, never, has value</td>
                        <td>Matches against the most recent DHCP lease timestamp on any interface; <em>never</em> finds hosts that have never received a DHCP lease</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td>Last Auth</td>
                        <td>after, before, between, never, has value</td>
                        <td>Matches against the most recent ClearPass authentication timestamp on any interface</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td>Switch IP</td>
                        <td>contains, starts with, ends with, equals, and negatives</td>
                        <td>IP address of the switch port the interface was last seen on (populated from ClearPass auth logs); supports wildcards</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td>Switch Port</td>
                        <td>contains, starts with, ends with, equals, and negatives</td>
                        <td>Port ID of the switch port the interface was last seen on (populated from ClearPass auth logs); supports wildcards</td>
                    </tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>
        <p class="mb-0 text-muted small">Search state (including the full condition builder query) is saved per user and restored automatically on your next visit.</p>
    </div>
</div>

{# Adding a host #}
<div class="card mb-4" id="add-host">
    <div class="card-header fw-semibold"><i class="bi bi-plus-circle me-1"></i>Adding a Host</div>
    <div class="card-body">
        <p>Click <strong>Add Host</strong> in the top-right of the host list (or the Dashboard). Fill in:</p>
        <dl class="row mb-0">
            <dt class="col-sm-3">Name <span class="text-danger">*</span></dt>
            <dd class="col-sm-9">The hostname for this device. Should match what you'd use in DNS.</dd>
            <dt class="col-sm-3">Building</dt>
            <dd class="col-sm-9">Physical building where the device lives (optional, for searching).</dd>
            <dt class="col-sm-3">Room</dt>
            <dd class="col-sm-9">Room number or label within the building.</dd>
            <dt class="col-sm-3">Tags</dt>
            <dd class="col-sm-9">One or more labels for grouping and filtering. Start typing to search existing tags.</dd>
            <dt class="col-sm-3">Notes</dt>
            <dd class="col-sm-9">Free-text notes visible on the host detail page.</dd>
        </dl>
        <div class="alert alert-info mt-3 mb-0 py-2 small">
            <i class="bi bi-lightbulb me-1"></i>After saving the host, you'll be redirected to the host detail page where you can add network interfaces.
        </div>
    </div>
</div>

{# Interfaces #}
<div class="card mb-4" id="interfaces">
    <div class="card-header fw-semibold"><i class="bi bi-hdd-network me-1"></i>Network Interfaces</div>
    <div class="card-body">
        <p>Each interface represents a physical or logical network port. From the host detail page, click <strong>Add Interface</strong> to create one. The header also has <strong>Edit</strong> and <strong>Delete</strong> buttons, and a <strong>View Logs</strong> button (<i class="bi bi-clock-history"></i>) that opens the Activity Log pre-filtered to show all events for that host and its interfaces, virtual IPs, DNS records, and API token — including events for entities that were later deleted or unlinked.</p>
        <dl class="row mb-0">
            <dt class="col-sm-3">MAC Address <span class="text-danger">*</span></dt>
            <dd class="col-sm-9">The hardware address of the interface, in <code>aa:bb:cc:dd:ee:ff</code> format. Used by DHCP for host reservations.</dd>
            <dt class="col-sm-3">Name</dt>
            <dd class="col-sm-9">Optional label (e.g. <code>eth0</code>, <code>Management</code>).</dd>
            <dt class="col-sm-3">Subnet</dt>
            <dd class="col-sm-9">The subnet this interface belongs to. Required before assigning an IP address.</dd>
            <dt class="col-sm-3">IPv4 Address</dt>
            <dd class="col-sm-9">Choose <strong>Auto-assign</strong> to use the next available IP in the subnet, or enter a specific address.</dd>
            <dt class="col-sm-3">IPv6 Address</dt>
            <dd class="col-sm-9">Optional. Choose auto-assign (uses EUI-64 generation or next available), IPv4-mapped, or manual entry.</dd>
        </dl>

        <h6 class="mt-4">DNS Records</h6>
        <p>After saving an interface, use the <strong>DNS Records</strong> section on the interface detail page to add DNS records for that interface. Click <strong>Add Record</strong> to create a new record. Each record has:</p>
        <ul class="mb-2">
            <li><strong>Domain</strong> — the DNS zone this record belongs to (only zones compatible with the interface's subnet are selectable)</li>
            <li><strong>Hostname</strong> — the name part without the domain suffix (e.g. <code>web01</code>)</li>
            <li><strong>Type</strong> — <code>A</code>, <code>AAAA</code>, <code>TXT</code>, or any other supported record type. Select <code>A+AAAA</code> to create both an A and an AAAA record simultaneously using the same hostname — the IPv4 address populates the A record and the IPv6 address populates the AAAA record. If the interface has no IPv6 address, only the A record is created and a warning is shown.</li>
            <li><strong>Value</strong> — leave blank for <code>A</code>/<code>AAAA</code> records; the IP address is derived automatically from the interface</li>
            <li><strong>Canonical</strong> — marks this record as the reverse DNS (PTR) name for the interface's IP. The first <code>A</code> record added to an interface is automatically marked canonical (used for IPv4 PTR); the first <code>AAAA</code> record is automatically canonical for IPv6. An interface can have at most one canonical <code>A</code> and one canonical <code>AAAA</code>.</li>
            <li><strong>Views</strong> — optionally restrict which DNS views include this record</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="text-muted small mb-0">Records with the <span class="badge bg-primary">PTR</span> badge are canonical and appear in reverse DNS zones. All interface-linked records appear in the domain's unified record list with a <em>Host</em> column showing the host name.</p>
        <p class="text-muted small mb-0 mt-2">In the <strong>host list</strong>, the DNS Names column shows a type badge before each hostname: <span class="badge text-bg-info">A</span> for IPv4-only, <span class="badge text-bg-success">AAAA</span> for IPv6-only, or <span class="badge text-bg-secondary">A+AAAA</span> when both an A and AAAA record exist for the same hostname — in which case the pair is shown as a single line.</p>
    </div>
</div>

{# DHCP leases #}
<div class="card mb-4" id="leases">
    <div class="card-header fw-semibold"><i class="bi bi-journal-text me-1"></i>DHCP Leases on a Host</div>
    <div class="card-body">
        <p>If DashDDI is receiving lease notifications from your Kea DHCP server, the host detail page shows the <strong>current active lease</strong> for each interface (alongside the static IP, if one is assigned). A lease IP shown in parentheses means it differs from the static assignment.</p>
        <p class="mb-0">Full lease history is available under <strong>DHCP → DHCP Leases</strong>.</p>
    </div>
</div>

{# Bulk operations #}
<div class="card mb-4" id="bulk">
    <div class="card-header fw-semibold"><i class="bi bi-check2-square me-1"></i>Bulk Operations</div>
    <div class="card-body">
        <p>The action bar above the table is always visible and combines controls for both host and interface selections.</p>

        <h6>Selecting rows</h6>
        <ul>
            <li>Check the checkbox on any <strong>host row</strong> to select that host, or on any <strong>interface sub-row</strong> to select that interface.</li>
            <li>Use the <strong>host select-all</strong> checkbox in the header to select every host on the current page, and the <strong>interface select-all</strong> checkbox to select every interface on the current page.</li>
            <li><strong>Shift-click</strong> a checkbox to select a range — hold Shift and click a second checkbox of the same type (host or interface) to select everything between them. Shift-clicking across types does nothing.</li>
            <li>Click <strong>Clear Selections</strong> to deselect everything at once.</li>
        </ul>

        <h6>Host bulk actions</h6>
        <p>Appear in the action bar when one or more hosts are selected:</p>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Add Tag</strong> — apply one or more tags to all selected hosts. You can also create a new tag from this modal.</li>
            <li><strong>Remove Tag</strong> — remove one or more tags from all selected hosts.</li>
            <li><strong>Delete Selected Hosts</strong> — soft-delete all selected hosts and their interfaces.</li>
        </ul>

        <h6>Interface bulk actions</h6>
        <p>Appear in the action bar when one or more interfaces are selected:</p>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Edit Selected Interfaces</strong> — opens a modal to reassign subnet and/or auto-assign new IPv4/IPv6 addresses across all selected interfaces at once. If an interface already has a valid IPv4 in the target subnet, it is left unchanged; IPv6 auto-assign (EUI-64 or last-octet) always recalculates.</li>
            <li><strong>Delete Selected Interfaces</strong> — soft-delete all selected interfaces.</li>
        </ul>

        <p class="mb-0 text-muted small">Bulk operations apply only to the selected rows on the current page, not the entire search result.</p>
    </div>
</div>

{# CSV Import #}
<div class="card mb-4" id="csv-import">
    <div class="card-header fw-semibold"><i class="bi bi-upload me-1"></i>Importing from CSV</div>
    <div class="card-body">
        <p>Use <strong>Hosts → Import CSV</strong> to bulk-create hosts and their interfaces from a spreadsheet. The import follows an upload → preview → confirm workflow so you can review changes before anything is written.</p>
        <h6>CSV format</h6>
        <p>Each row represents one interface. Rows that share the same <code>hostname</code> are merged into a single host with multiple interfaces. Download the blank template from the import page for the exact column order.</p>
        <p>Required columns: <code>hostname</code>, <code>mac_address</code>. All other columns are optional.</p>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>building</strong> — matched to an existing building by name (case-insensitive); left blank if not found.</li>
            <li><strong>tags</strong> — semicolon-separated tag names (e.g. <code>tag1;tag2</code>); unknown tags are created automatically.</li>
            <li><strong>subnet</strong> — CIDR notation (e.g. <code>192.168.1.0/24</code>); must match an existing subnet.</li>
            <li><strong>ip_address</strong> — a specific IPv4 address, or <code>auto</code> to assign the next available address in the subnet.</li>
            <li><strong>ipv6_address</strong> — a specific IPv6 address, <code>auto</code> to assign the next available address, or <code>auto_v4</code> to derive the address from the assigned IPv4.</li>
            <li><strong>dns_name</strong> — a fully-qualified domain name (e.g. <code>myhost.example.com</code>); the longest matching known domain is used. DNS views are selected automatically based on the subnet and domain.</li>
        </ul>
        <h6>Conflict handling</h6>
        <p>The import is all-or-nothing: <strong>any issue blocks the entire import</strong>. Fix the CSV and re-upload to proceed. Issues that block import:</p>
        <ul>
            <li>Blank or invalid MAC address (caught at parse time with a line number).</li>
            <li>Hostname already exists as a live (non-deleted) host.</li>
            <li>MAC address already assigned to a live interface.</li>
            <li>Subnet CIDR does not match any existing subnet.</li>
            <li>DNS name does not match any known domain.</li>
            <li>IP address already assigned to a live interface, or duplicated within the file.</li>
        </ul>
        <h6>Import date tag</h6>
        <p>Every import run automatically creates and applies a tag of the form <code>import:YYYY-MM-DD</code> to all imported hosts. If more than one import runs on the same day, a numeric suffix is added (<code>import:2026-06-23:2</code>, <code>import:2026-06-23:3</code>, …) so each batch gets a distinct tag. The tag name is shown in the confirmation message after import.</p>
        <h6>Replacing soft-deleted hosts</h6>
        <p class="mb-0">If a hostname in the CSV matches a <strong>soft-deleted</strong> host, the import treats it as available and creates a fresh host in its place. IP addresses previously held by the deleted host's interfaces are also freed automatically, so those addresses can be re-assigned to the new host without conflict.</p>
    </div>
</div>

{# Soft delete #}
<div class="card mb-4" id="soft-delete">
    <div class="card-header fw-semibold"><i class="bi bi-trash me-1"></i>Deleted Hosts &amp; Restore</div>
    <div class="card-body">
        <p>When you delete a host, it is <strong>soft-deleted</strong> — marked as deleted but not removed from the database. Soft-deleted hosts are hidden from normal views and excluded from DNS and DHCP generation.</p>
        <p>To see deleted hosts, open the <strong>Advanced</strong> search and add a <strong>Deleted — is true</strong> condition. From those results you can:</p>
        <ul class="mb-0">
            <li><strong>Restore</strong> — click the restore icon (<i class="bi bi-arrow-counterclockwise"></i>) to bring the host back to active status</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="mt-2 mb-0 text-muted small">Deleted hosts are permanently purged automatically based on the <em>lease retention</em> setting configured on each subnet, or after a default period. Check <strong>Application Settings</strong> for the global default.</p>
    </div>
</div>

{# Virtual IPs #}
<div class="card mb-4" id="virtual-ips">
    <div class="card-header fw-semibold"><i class="bi bi-layers me-1"></i>Virtual IPs (VRRP / HSRP / Active-Gateway)</div>
    <div class="card-body">
        <p>A <strong>Virtual IP</strong> (VIP) is a floating IP address shared by a group of physical routers using a redundancy protocol such as VRRP, HSRP, or an Active-Gateway (as used in Aruba CX). Unlike a regular interface address, a VIP is not bound to a single host — it moves automatically between the member routers when a failover occurs.</p>

        <h6 class="mt-3">Creating a Virtual IP</h6>
        <p>Navigate to a subnet and click <strong>Add Virtual IP</strong>. Fill in:</p>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Label</strong> — a descriptive name, e.g. <em>core-switch-gateway</em></li>
            <li><strong>Protocol</strong> — VRRP, HSRP, Active-Gateway, or Other</li>
            <li><strong>VRID / Group ID</strong> — optional group number (1–255)</li>
            <li><strong>Member Interfaces</strong> — the physical interfaces that participate in the redundancy group; select multiple with Ctrl/Cmd</li>
            <li><strong>IPv4 / IPv6 Address</strong> — assign automatically from the available pool, enter a specific address, or leave unassigned</li>
            <li><strong>Notes</strong> — any free-form notes</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="text-muted small">VIP IP addresses are tracked in IPAM the same way as interface addresses — they are excluded from the "available" pool and will not be auto-assigned to new interfaces.</p>

        <h6 class="mt-3">Managing VIPs from the subnet page</h6>
        <p>The subnet detail page lists all VIPs in a <strong>Virtual IPs</strong> section. Each row has an edit button (<i class="bi bi-pencil"></i>) to open the VIP edit form and a delete button (<i class="bi bi-trash"></i>) to soft-delete the VIP.</p>

        <h6 class="mt-3">Managing VIPs from the interface page</h6>
        <p>The interface detail page has a <strong>Virtual IPs</strong> section listing every VIP group that interface belongs to. Actions on each row:</p>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Edit</strong> (<i class="bi bi-pencil"></i>) — open the VIP edit form</li>
            <li><strong>Unlink</strong> (<i class="bi bi-dash-circle"></i>) — remove this interface from the VIP group without deleting the VIP</li>
            <li><strong>Delete</strong> (<i class="bi bi-trash"></i>) — soft-delete the VIP entirely</li>
        </ul>
        <p>You can also add the interface to an existing VIP or create a new VIP directly from the interface page using the <strong>Link to existing VIP</strong> and <strong>Create new VIP</strong> buttons.</p>

        <h6 class="mt-3">DNS Records for Virtual IPs</h6>
        <p>The VIP detail page has a <strong>DNS Records</strong> section. Click <strong>Add Record</strong> to attach a new DNS record to the VIP, or link an existing record using the <strong>Linked to</strong> search field on the record edit form. For <strong>A</strong> and <strong>AAAA</strong> records the value is derived automatically from the VIP's assigned IP — you leave the value blank. Marking a record as <strong>Canonical</strong> causes it to be used as the PTR hostname in reverse DNS zones, exactly as it works for interface records. Each record row has an unlink button (<i class="bi bi-slash-circle"></i>) to detach the record from the VIP without deleting it, and a delete button (<i class="bi bi-trash"></i>) to remove the record entirely.</p>

        <h6 class="mt-3">Audit trail</h6>
        <p class="mb-0">The VIP detail page shows a <strong>Last Modified</strong> line with the date, time, and username of the last change — the same audit information shown on hosts and interfaces.</p>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="card mb-4" id="ssh-host-keys">
    <div class="card-header fw-semibold"><i class="bi bi-shield-lock me-1"></i>SSH Host Key Pinning</div>
    <div class="card-body">
        <p>DashDDI connects to DNS and DHCP servers over SSH to push configuration files. To protect against man-in-the-middle attacks, it pins the SSH host key for each server using a <strong>Trust On First Use (TOFU)</strong> policy: the key presented by the server on the first connection is stored and verified on every subsequent connection.</p>

        <h6 class="mt-3">How TOFU works</h6>
        <p>The first time DashDDI connects to a server, it saves the host key automatically — no action is required. On every subsequent connection, it compares the presented key against the stored value. If they do not match, the connection is refused and a descriptive error is logged. This prevents an attacker who has intercepted the network path from transparently proxying the SSH session.</p>
        <p class="text-muted small">Keys are stored per algorithm (e.g. <code>ssh-ed25519</code>, <code>ecdsa-sha2-nistp256</code>). A host may have multiple stored keys — one per algorithm — to support servers that advertise more than one key type.</p>

        <h6 class="mt-3">Viewing stored keys</h6>
        <p>The <strong>SSH Host Keys</strong> card on the host detail page lists every stored key for that host, showing the algorithm and the SHA-256 fingerprint. Use the fingerprint to verify the key against the server's own record:</p>
        <pre class="small bg-body-secondary rounded p-2">ssh-keygen -lf /etc/ssh/ssh_host_ed25519_key.pub</pre>

        <h6 class="mt-3">Adding keys automatically (ssh-keyscan)</h6>
        <p>Click <strong>Scan with ssh-keyscan</strong> on the host detail page. DashDDI will run <code>ssh-keyscan</code> against the host's first IPv4 address and import all returned keys. The scan uses a 5-second timeout and will report an error if the host is unreachable or not running SSH. This is the easiest way to pre-load keys before the first automated connection, or to add keys for additional algorithms.</p>

        <h6 class="mt-3">Adding keys manually</h6>
        <p>Expand the <strong>Add / replace keys manually</strong> panel and paste one or more public host keys. Both formats are accepted:</p>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Bare key format</strong> — the contents of <code>/etc/ssh/ssh_host_*_key.pub</code>, e.g. <code>ssh-ed25519 AAAA…</code></li>
            <li><strong>ssh-keyscan output</strong> — lines that include a leading hostname token, e.g. <code>192.168.1.10 ssh-ed25519 AAAA…</code></li>
        </ul>
        <p>You can paste multiple lines at once to import several algorithms in one operation. If a key for the same algorithm already exists it is replaced.</p>

        <h6 class="mt-3">Removing or rotating keys</h6>
        <p>Each stored key has a <i class="bi bi-trash"></i> button on the host detail page. Removing a key causes the next connection to treat that algorithm as a first-use and re-learn the key automatically. This is the correct procedure when a server's host key has been legitimately regenerated (e.g. after an OS reinstall).</p>

        <p class="text-muted small mb-0"><strong>Note:</strong> SSH host key pinning applies to connections DashDDI makes outbound — DNS deploy, DHCP deploy, and Aruba CX switch management. It does not apply to inbound API or SAML connections.</p>
    </div>
</div>

